{"subjectId":"python","id":"os-sys","title":"os, sys, shutil & subprocess","group":"Standard Library","order":52,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The modules that let a Python program talk to its environment: the interpreter (<code>sys</code>), the operating system (<code>os</code>), whole files and folders (<code>shutil</code>) and other programs (<code>subprocess</code>).</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brand-python\"></i>sys — the interpreter</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import sys\n\nprint(sys.version_info &gt;= (3, 10))          # True\nprint(type(sys.argv).__name__)               # list   ← [\"script.py\", \"arg1\", ...]\nprint(isinstance(sys.path, list))            # True   ← where imports are searched\nprint(sys.maxsize == 2**63 - 1)              # True   ← on 64-bit builds\nprint(sys.getrecursionlimit())               # 1000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>sys.argv</code></td><td>Command-line arguments; <code>argv[0]</code> is the script name</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys.exit(code)</code></td><td>Exit by raising <code>SystemExit</code> — 0 means success</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys.stdin</code> / <code>stdout</code> / <code>stderr</code></td><td>The standard streams (file objects)</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys.path</code></td><td>Module search path</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys.modules</code></td><td>Cache of already-imported modules</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys.platform</code></td><td><code>'linux'</code>, <code>'darwin'</code>, <code>'win32'</code>, …</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys.executable</code></td><td>Path of the running Python interpreter</td></tr><tr><td><code>sys.getsizeof(obj)</code></td><td>Shallow size of an object in bytes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-settings\"></i>os — environment, paths, processes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import os\n\nos.environ[\"APP_MODE\"] = \"test\"                     # visible to child processes too\nprint(os.environ.get(\"APP_MODE\"))                   # test\nprint(os.environ.get(\"NOT_SET_ANYWHERE\", \"default\"))  # default\nprint(os.getenv(\"NOT_SET_ANYWHERE\") is None)        # True\nprint(os.path.join(\"data\", \"2026\", \"report.csv\"))   # data/2026/report.csv\nprint(os.path.splitext(\"photo.tar.gz\"))             # ('photo.tar', '.gz')\nprint(os.sep, os.cpu_count() &gt; 0)                   # / True\ndel os.environ[\"APP_MODE\"]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>os.environ[\"KEY\"]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code> when the variable is missing — use <code>os.environ.get(\"KEY\", default)</code> for optional settings, and never print secrets pulled from the environment.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-folders\"></i>Walking a directory tree</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import os, tempfile\n\nroot = tempfile.mkdtemp()\nfor rel in [\"src/app.py\", \"src/util.py\", \"docs/index.md\", \"README.md\"]:\n    path = os.path.join(root, rel)\n    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)\n    open(path, \"w\").close()\n\nprint(sorted(os.listdir(root)))                      # ['README.md', 'docs', 'src']\nwith os.scandir(root) as entries:                    # faster: file type info included\n    print(sorted(e.name for e in entries if e.is_dir()))   # ['docs', 'src']\n\nfound = []\nfor dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):\n    found += [f for f in filenames if f.endswith(\".py\")]\nprint(sorted(found))                                 # ['app.py', 'util.py']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>For new code, <code>pathlib.Path</code> (see <em>Files, JSON &amp; pathlib</em>) is usually nicer: <code>Path(root).rglob(\"*.py\")</code> replaces the <code>os.walk</code> loop.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>shutil — high-level file operations</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import shutil, tempfile\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nbase = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp())\n(base / \"src\").mkdir()\n(base / \"src\" / \"a.txt\").write_text(\"hello\")\n\nshutil.copy(base / \"src\" / \"a.txt\", base / \"b.txt\")        # copy one file\nshutil.copytree(base / \"src\", base / \"backup\")             # copy a whole tree\nshutil.move(base / \"b.txt\", base / \"c.txt\")                # move / rename\nshutil.rmtree(base / \"src\")                                # delete a tree — careful!\nprint(sorted(p.name for p in base.iterdir()))              # ['backup', 'c.txt']\nprint(shutil.which(\"definitely-not-a-real-cmd\"))           # None\narchive = shutil.make_archive(str(base / \"bundle\"), \"zip\", base / \"backup\")\nprint(Path(archive).name)                                  # bundle.zip</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-play\"></i>subprocess — running other programs</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import subprocess, sys\n\nresult = subprocess.run(\n    [sys.executable, \"-c\", \"print('hello from a child process')\"],\n    capture_output=True, text=True,\n)\nprint(result.returncode, result.stdout.strip())   # 0 hello from a child process\n\ntry:\n    subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", \"raise SystemExit(2)\"], check=True)\nexcept subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:\n    print(\"failed with\", e.returncode)            # failed with 2\n\nout = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", \"import sys; print(sys.stdin.read().upper())\"],\n                     input=\"piped text\", capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)\nprint(out.stdout.strip())                          # PIPED TEXT</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Argument</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>[\"cmd\", \"arg\"]</code> (a list)</td><td>Runs the program directly — no shell, no quoting problems</td></tr><tr><td><code>capture_output=True</code></td><td>Collect stdout / stderr instead of printing them</td></tr><tr><td><code>text=True</code></td><td>Decode bytes to <code>str</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>check=True</code></td><td>Raise <code>CalledProcessError</code> on a non-zero exit code</td></tr><tr><td><code>input=…</code></td><td>Send data to the child's stdin</td></tr><tr><td><code>timeout=…</code></td><td>Kill the child and raise <code>TimeoutExpired</code> if it takes too long</td></tr><tr><td><code>cwd=…</code>, <code>env=…</code></td><td>Working directory / environment for the child</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Shell injection</div><p>Never build a command string from user input with <code>shell=True</code> — <code>f\"ls {name}\"</code> with <code>name = \"x; rm -rf ~\"</code> runs both commands. Pass a list of arguments instead.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between os and sys?</summary><div><p><code>sys</code> is about the Python interpreter itself — arguments, import path, standard streams, exit, recursion limit. <code>os</code> is about the operating system — environment variables, files and directories, processes, permissions.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you run a shell command from Python and get its output?</summary><div><p><code>subprocess.run([\"git\", \"status\"], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout</code>. Use a list of arguments (no <code>shell=True</code>), <code>check=True</code> to fail loudly, and <code>timeout</code> for anything that might hang.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does sys.exit() work?</summary><div><p>It raises <code>SystemExit</code>. If nothing catches it, the interpreter exits with that code (0 = success, non-zero = error; a string is printed to stderr and gives exit code 1). <code>finally</code> blocks and <code>with</code> exits still run.</p></div></details></section>"}